<!--
  Network Name: isolated_network_32

  This network uses a Class A private subnet 10.32.0.0/24, inside 10.0.0.0/8:
    - 10.32.0.0      -> The "network address" used to identify the entire subnet. NetID.
                        It cannot be assigned to any host or device.
    - 10.32.0.1      -> Assigned to the libvirt bridge 'virbr2' on the host.
                        Isolated-only guests use this as their default
                        gateway so they can reply to Silenus. There is still
                        no masquerade, so the public internet does not answer.
    - 10.32.0.2
      through
      10.32.0.254    -> Usable range for VM static IPs (no DHCP configured).
    - 10.32.0.255    -> The "broadcast address" for this subnet. Also not assignable to any host.

  Mode: isolated
    - There is no <forward> element and no masquerade. Guests on this bridge
      reach each other and this host. They have no path to the public
      internet. Silenus routes 10.32.0.0/24 the same way it routes
      192.168.32.0/24; this host holds a route back to 10.24.0.0/24.
      guest-net-access lets private networks into virbr2 and lets this subnet
      reply.
    - The same host numbers as the NAT networks (24, 32, 40) are reused in
      10.0.0.0/8 so an address still names the machine it belongs to.

  No DHCP:
    - No <dhcp> section is defined, so each VM must be manually configured with a static IP
      (e.g., 10.32.0.10, netmask=255.255.255.0, gateway=10.32.0.1).

  Typical VM static configuration might look like:
    IP Address: 10.32.0.10
    Netmask:    255.255.255.0
    Gateway:    10.32.0.1
    DNS:        (none)

  Dual-homed guests keep their default gateway on static_network_32
  (192.168.32.1) and put 10.32.0.0/24 on a second NIC attached here.

  The NAT network on this host is static_network_32. libvirt's own 'default'
  network is removed in Step 7 rather than left stopped.

  Silenus uses the same pattern on 10.24.0.0/24. The subnets differ on purpose:
  the two hosts are reachable from each other, so overlapping guest ranges would
  make a guest on one indistinguishable from a guest on the other.

    virsh -c qemu:///system net-define    kvm/isolated_network_32.xml
    virsh -c qemu:///system net-start     isolated_network_32
    virsh -c qemu:///system net-autostart isolated_network_32
-->

<network>
  <name>isolated_network_32</name>
  <bridge name="virbr2" stp="on" delay="0"/>
  <ip address="10.32.0.1" netmask="255.255.255.0">
  </ip>
</network>
